    'stock-evaluation-criteria',
    'Stock evaluation criteria',
    'Why Establish Investment Criteria? When investing in individual company stocks, it’s essential to have a clear set of criteria for evaluating a stock’s potential. These criteria act as guardrails, helping investors stay objective and avoid making emotional decisions. For example, during periods of…',
    '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why Establish Investment Criteria?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When investing in individual company stocks, it’s essential to have a clear set of criteria for evaluating a stock’s potential. These criteria act as guardrails, helping investors stay objective and avoid making emotional decisions. For example, during periods of hype around meme stocks or when FOMO (fear of missing out) is at its peak, pre-established guidelines offer a rational framework for decision-making. Additionally, regularly reviewing investments against these criteria can inform decisions about when to sell or when to revise outdated approaches."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Constitutes Investment Criteria?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before outlining specific criteria, it’s important to define what qualifies. Investment criteria go beyond simple screening filters. For instance, a price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio under 20 is not sufficient on its own. Instead, criteria should represent broader concepts that are consistently applicable while remaining as specific as possible. Using the P/E ratio as an example, a more meaningful criterion might be that the stock is undervalued by at least 20% relative to its fair valuation, even if the method for determining fair value varies by company."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"My Investment Criteria"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is my personal list of investment criteria, along with the rationale behind each. These have evolved over time and will likely continue to adapt as I gain more experience. My criteria address both the company and its stock."}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Net Income Positive","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":"A company’s value is ultimately tied to its ability to generate cash. I prioritise companies that have demonstrated their capacity to produce positive returns. While this doesn’t guarantee scalability or sustainability, it shows the business model’s viability. Although this approach might miss out on early growth phases, I believe the risk/reward profile is generally better once this milestone is achieved. Valuation methods such as discounted cash flow (DCF) and earnings per share (EPS) rely on positive income; without it, substantial guesswork is required."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Attractive Valuation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":"Ideally, I aim to invest in exceptional companies at significant discounts. However, given current market conditions, this isn’t always possible. I consider investments that meet one of the following: a great company at a fair valuation or a fair company at a great valuation. While Warren Buffett famously said it’s better to buy a great company at a fair price than a fair company at a great price, the rapid pace of technological disruption has increasingly challenged even the strongest companies. Examples such as Blockbuster, General Motors, and Toys “R” Us illustrate this point. As such, I value both scenarios under the broader concept of “attractive valuation”."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shareholder-Friendly Management","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":"I look for companies that consistently return value to shareholders. This includes avoiding excessive share dilution, as it directly reduces the ownership proportion per share. Even if the overall share count doesn’t increase, high levels of stock-based compensation can indicate a company is using significant cash flow to offset this expense. While I recognise this may be necessary in competitive industries to retain talent, excessive dilution is a concern. On the positive side, buybacks and dividends are favourable, with consistent buybacks having an edge due to the flexibility they provide. Unlike dividends, which are taxed before reinvestment, buybacks increase the stock’s value, allowing investors to decide when to incur tax on sales."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sector Diversification and Exclusions","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":"This criterion is influenced by the composition of my broader portfolio. I believe it’s prudent to diversify across at least 3–4 sectors, so individual investments that enhance diversification are preferred over those that create excessive concentration in a single sector. This approach helps mitigate risks from unpredictable macroeconomic events, such as natural disasters or major legislative changes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clean Balance Sheet","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"text","text":"A strong balance sheet ensures a company has the flexibility to adapt to sudden changes in its business environment. Highly leveraged companies face significant challenges in responding to new circumstances due to their debt burden. I prefer companies with a (total debt – cash) / EBITDA ratio of 3 or less, as this indicates manageable leverage."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Disclaimer"},{"type":"text","text":"*"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The information provided here is not financial advice and should not be interpreted as such. I am not a financial advisor, and this is a record of my personal investment journey and decisions. Always conduct your own research before making investment decisions."}]}]}'::jsonb,
